WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.740
McElligott: It'd be great if you could tell us 
what it's like on site.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:07.523
Are all the observations firsthand?

00:00:07.523 --> 00:00:09.760
Are there cameras around?

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.060
What's the nature, what's the size,
what's the nature of the conditions?

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.467
Lonsdorf: When you are, this is
how it works at Gombe,

00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:21.000
it's a little bit different at Karisoke in Rwanda

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.180
because they're, they have limited time with the gorillas.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.740
But what I do when I'm at Gombe

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.780
is that I wake up very early in the morning.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.480
We know where the chimps have slept the night before

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.460
because they actually build a night nest in the trees,

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:34.980
and if you follow them

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.320
until late in the day when they build that nest

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.100
you're like,
"Great, that's where you'll be at 6AM tomorrow."

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.570
So you wake up so that you're with them at 6AM.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.000
And what our on-site researchers do is

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.860
they follow a single kind of focal individual all day,

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:51.440
from the time they get up till the time they go to bed.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.780
And they're taking a series of consistent data
every 15 minutes.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.960
But then taking longhand data

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:03.820
throughout the day about interesting things like mating and grooming and fighting and all that kind of stuff.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.340
So you're on foot in the  morning,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:08.980
a chimp comes down from the tree.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.800
Wake up time is a really good time
to collect fecal samples, by the way.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.320
Just makes sense if you think about it, right?

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.500
So you get there in the morning,

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.880
collect all your samples.
Then you're with the chimps all day.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.200
You're following them at a distance of about 7 meters.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.120
That's what we consider a safe distance for them

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.660
and for us. They break it often

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.420
because you're either in their way or they're curious.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.200
So like me to the fourth row.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.780
And you're with them and recording
everything they do all day.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.100
Now we have several teams out,
we have a team focused on the adults.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.200
And we have a specific team focused on
mothers and kids as part of this grant.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.120
So there's gonna be multiple teams out in the day

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.980
following individual chimpanzees around.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:53.480
Documenting their behavior

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.780
where they go, what they eat.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.340
Collecting samples all day.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.160
And that has been going on
essentially unchanged since 1966.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:05.646
McElligott: And you're based in Pennsylvania but you're out there...?

00:02:05.646 --> 00:02:09.239
Lonsdorf: I'm out there once a year.

00:02:09.239 --> 00:02:10.900
When I was a student, I could go more.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.680
But then I invested in my own offspring and it all went downhill from there.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:20.701
McElligott: These expensive children. We've got another question in the
back there.

00:02:20.701 --> 00:02:23.700
QUESTION: Thanks very much for the talk. I'm interested in childbirth,

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:30.730
Could you talk a little bit about it?
Sociology, where the mother goes?

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:35.440
McElligott: Just make sure you're holding the mic closer we can hear you.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:46.680
QUESTION: Childbirth...how many individuals are around when the infant is born. First treatments...

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.000
Lonsdorf: The question is about childbirth and the act of childbirth

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.187
and how that happens in apes and in chimpanzees.

00:02:55.187 --> 00:03:02.580
Interestingly enough, a colleague of mine is finalizing
a paper and analysis of that right now.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.180
But it's highly variable.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:11.520
So the important thing to point out about chimpanzees is that they are extremely aggressive

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.230
within their own community.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.780
And infanticide is a real risk.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:22.780
From stranger chimpanzees, but also from chimpanzees in your community,

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.080
both males and females.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.600
So a newborn baby is incredibly vulnerable.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.800
Its mother is subject to infanticidal attacks.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:37.220
So because of that what often happens is that
mom goes on a bit of a maternity leave

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.660
and wants to be by herself.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.780
When she gives birth to that baby.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:47.380
Now we of course have outliers. We have a very—had, she's passed away now—a very dominant female

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:51.880
named Fifi who had more babies than anybody else.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.720
She's the one in that picture touching Jane.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.560
She's kind of the best documented animal on the planet.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.680
Very comfortable in her community
and also with humans.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.310
And she would give birth like right in front of tourists.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:14.940
Her sons were always alpha male,
she wasn't worried about this infanticidal thing.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.780
McElligott: Rock star behavior
Lonsdorf: Yeah. But typically

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:20.660
there is a real maternity leave. And we don't know much about it.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.980
Because if they decide to be
where we can't find them, we can't find them.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:29.000
So I can't answer your question really well,
because we don't get to see it very much.

